  * changes is underneath -- the Arduino HardwareSerial (127-byte ring, one byte per
  * interrupt, one byte per read()) is gone, so a request frame larger than that ring is no
  * longer at the mercy of how fast the receive task is scheduled.
+ *
+ * ---- LINK-CTRL (issue #23 U0-3) ----------------------------------------------
+ *
+ * receive() is overridden to multiplex a SECOND frame type onto the same wire, handled
+ * here in the link layer and never passed up to eRPC:
+ *
+ *   CTRL frame = u16 0xFFFF | u16 body_len | u16 crc16(body) | body[body_len]
+ *   body       = u8 cmd | u8 seq | u8 status | u8 rsvd | payload[]
+ *
+ * 0xFFFF is unambiguous because it is not a message size any sender can produce (the
+ * host's largest request is a few hundred bytes, ours is bounded by the 4 kB
+ * MessageBuffer).  It carries what eRPC cannot: reading this driver's counters (the LOG
+ * UART is unreachable while the host holds the link), changing the line rate, and
+ * generating measured traffic for the U0 go/no-go.  Doing it here rather than as eRPC
+ * methods avoids hand-editing the GENERATED server shim -- the same shim whose
+ * erpc_free() of a string literal corrupted the module heap in issue #20.
+ *
+ * Two locking rules, both load-bearing:
+ *   - the whole read, including CTRL consumption, stays under m_receiveLock, exactly as
+ *     the base class does (erpc_framed_transport.cpp);
+ *   - a CTRL reply is written under m_sendLock, because the N3 worker tasks call send()
+ *     concurrently and two writers would interleave their bytes.  This is the only place
+ *     that takes both, receive then send, and nothing takes them the other way round, so
+ *     the order cannot cycle.
